<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyu Ancient Cities</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyu Ancient Cities are three walled cities of the Pyu people in the Irrawaddy basin of Myanmar, Halin, Beikthano and Sri Ksetra, inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 2014. Flourishing from about 200 BCE to 900 CE, they contain brick walls, palaces, Buddhist stupas, burial grounds and water works. They show the early spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and urban life in the region before the rise of Bagan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyu-ancient-cities.jpg" alt="Pyu Ancient Cities" width="320" height="641" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanguole · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyu_city-states_physical_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
